From 470af3dde33cc52222fdf56038358d63b7b253e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:15:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] Move the PIN lockout counters where a transaction
 protects them

Both PIN registries kept failed_attempt_count inside their JSON store, and
every store in portal/models does read-modify-write on the whole file. Its own
helper says so: "Two processes doing read-modify-write against the same store
still lose one update; the last os.replace wins." That was accepted deliberately,
and for durability it is a reasonable trade.

It stops being reasonable when a lockout counter is the field being lost,
because then the lost update is the brute-force protection. Measured, with the
original read-modify-write shape and twelve concurrent failed attempts:

    OLD  12 concurrent failures recorded as  1   -> lockout at 5 never trips
    NEW  12 concurrent failures recorded as 12   -> locked

An attacker controls exactly the variable that defeats it -- how many requests
to send at once -- and nothing in the registry, the audit log or any screen
would show it. The counter simply stays low.

dispatch/authcounters.py holds the state in auth_attempt_state and increments it
with a single UPDATE inside a transaction, deciding the lockout in the same
statement rather than against a re-read count. Only the contended field moved:
PIN hashes, recovery words, creation, reset and revocation are untouched, and
_public() reads the two fields back so every caller sees the shape it always did.

Lockout expiry takes `now` as an argument, because a test that has to sleep
fifteen minutes to prove an expiry is a test nobody runs.
